          "filename": "Solar prominence from STEREO spacecraft September 29, 2008.jpg",
          "caption": "The STEREO (Ahead) spacecraft observed this visually stunning solar prominence eruption on September 29, 2008, in the 304 angstrom wavelength of extreme UV light. Prominences are relatively cool clouds of gas suspended above the sun and controlled by magnetic forces.\nThe prominence rose and cascaded to the right over several hours, appearing something like a flag unfurling, as it broke apart and headed into space. The prominence is composed of ionized Helium that is about 60,000 degrees Kelvin.",
